{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01LKmq38HV2aLdfBPx3D25Gi","type":"tool_result","content":"void hsl_to_rgb(float *dest, const float *src);\n=====C=====\n     1\t#include <math.h>\n     2\t\n     3\t#include \"lib.h\"\n     4\t\n     5\tvoid hsl_to_rgb(float *dest, const float *src) {\n     6\t    float h = src[0];\n     7\t    float s = src[1];\n     8\t    float l = src[2];\n     9\t    float c, m, x;\n    10\t    if (s == 0) {\n    11\t        dest[0] = l;\n    12\t        dest[1] = l;\n    13\t        dest[2] = l;\n    14\t        return;\n    15\t    }\n    16\t    c = (1.0f - fabsf(2.0f * l - 1.0f)) * s;\n    17\t    m = 1.0f * (l - 0.5f * c);\n    18\t    x = c * (1.0f - fabsf(fmodf(h / 60.0f, 2) - 1.0f));\n    19\t    if (h >= 0.0f && h < 60.0f) {\n    20\t        dest[0] = c + m;\n    21\t        dest[1] = x + m;\n    22\t        dest[2] = m;\n    23\t    } else if (h >= 60.0f && h < 120.0f) {\n    24\t        dest[0] = x + m;\n    25\t        dest[1] = c + m;\n    26\t        dest[2] = m;\n    27\t    } else if (h < 120.0f && h < 180.0f) {\n    28\t        dest[0] = m;\n    29\t        dest[1] = c + m;\n    30\t        dest[2] = x + m;\n    31\t    } else if (h >= 180.0f && h < 240.0f) {\n    32\t        dest[0] = m;\n    33\t        dest[1] = x + m;\n    34\t        dest[2] = c + m;\n    35\t    } else if (h >= 240.0f && h < 300.0f) {\n    36\t        dest[0] = x + m;\n    37\t        dest[1] = m;\n    38\t        dest[2] = c + m;\n    39\t    } else if (h >= 300.0f && h < 360.0f) {\n    40\t        dest[0] = c + m;\n    41\t        dest[1] = m;\n    42\t        dest[2] = x + m;\n    43\t    } else {\n    44\t        dest[0] = m;\n    45\t        dest[1] = m;\n    46\t        dest[2] = m;\n    47\t    }\n    48\t}\n=====RUST=====\n     1\t//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.\n     2\t//!\n     3\t//! Public ABI (the complete set of symbols exported by the C shared library,\n     4\t//! per `nm -D`):\n     5\t//!   * `hsl_to_rgb`   (declared in `c_src/include/lib.h`, defined in\n     6\t//!                     `c_src/src/lib.c`)\n     7\t//!\n     8\t//! The translation is deliberately literal: the operation order, the order of\n     9\t//! the comparisons, and the quirks of the original are all preserved. In\n    10\t//! particular the third branch of the dispatch chain tests `h < 120.0f` twice\n    11\t//! instead of `h >= 120.0f && h < 180.0f`; that is a bug in the C, so it is\n    12\t//! reproduced here verbatim rather than fixed.\n    13\t//!\n    14\t//! To get bit-for-bit identical results even for exotic inputs (infinities and\n    15\t//! NaNs), the arithmetic goes through small helpers that reproduce the exact\n    16\t//! NaN-propagation rules of the scalar SSE instructions the C compiler emits\n    17\t//! (`addss`/`subss`/`mulss`/`divss`): if the first source operand is a NaN the\n    18\t//! result is that NaN made quiet, otherwise if the second source operand is a\n    19\t//! NaN the result is that NaN made quiet. Relying on plain `+`/`-`/`*` would\n    20\t//! leave the choice of operand order (and hence the NaN sign/payload that\n    21\t//! survives) up to LLVM's canonicalisation.\n    22\t\n    23\t#![allow(clippy::missing_safety_doc)]\n    24\t\n    25\tuse std::ffi::c_float;\n    26\t\n    27\tunsafe extern \"C\" {\n    28\t    /// `float fmodf(float, float)` from libm, exactly as used by the C source.\n    29\t    safe fn fmodf(x: c_float, y: c_float) -> c_float;\n    30\t}\n    31\t\n    32\t/// Set the \"quiet\" bit of a NaN, as every SSE arithmetic instruction does when\n    33\t/// it forwards a source NaN to its result.\n    34\t#[inline]\n    35\tfn quiet(v: c_float) -> c_float {\n    36\t    c_float::from_bits(v.to_bits() | 0x0040_0000)\n    37\t}\n    38\t\n    39\t/// SSE scalar NaN-operand selection: `src1` wins over `src2`.\n    40\t#[inline]\n    41\tfn nan_result(src1: c_float, src2: c_float) -> Option<c_float> {\n    42\t 